Rangeify source fixtures
=======================

The reference driver uses tinygrad d0c9745274335e44b5dd7c15b8422c2b684a3ed9.
Generate into a separate directory before comparing or promoting expectations:

  DEV=CPU python3 packages/tolk/test/golden/rangeify/generate_expected.py \
    --tinygrad _plans/tinygrad-d0c974527 --output _plans/goldens-d0/rangeify
  DEV=CPU _build/default/packages/tolk/test/golden/rangeify/generate_actual.exe \
    _plans/goldens-d0/rangeify

The ordinary cases compare tensor-level graph lowering and rendered source.
The four isolated Llama cases retain individual model-derived kernel graphs.
The full llama_forward_from_embedding case instead builds the complete tensor
computation and lets the shared scheduler determine the kernel boundaries.

That forward graph matches extra/models/llama.py's Transformer with dim=8,
hidden_dim=16, n_heads=2, n_kv_heads=1, n_layers=1, norm_eps=1e-5,
vocab_size=32, max_context=8, jit=False, and disable_kv_cache=True, starting
from float32 embeddings of shape (1, 2, 8). It includes RMS normalization,
rotary embeddings, causal grouped-query attention, residuals, the gated feed
forward block, its output clone, and the final normalized projection. Both
drivers schedule the logits, block output, and all model parameter tensors
together. The reference replaces all model tensors with empty inputs,
including freqs_cis of shape (1, 16, 1, 2, 2); the OCaml graph does the same.

These are source comparisons for Clang, CUDA, Metal, and OpenCL renderers.
They do not demonstrate numerical execution on those accelerator backends.

The private Llama_fixture builder is also exercised numerically by test_llama.
Its target CPU values use norm weights equal to 1, rotary (cos, sin) pairs
(1, 0), all other weights ((flat_index mod 13) - 6) / 16, and embeddings
(flat_index - 7) / 8. The test compares every element of the block output and
final logits, with absolute tolerance 2e-5, using Tolk's normal CPU execution
path. Run it with:

  DEV=CPU _build/default/packages/tolk/test/golden/rangeify/test_llama.exe

Raw .expected files retain the unchanged reference output. Adjacent
.expected.diff files cover the reviewed IEEE differences in DIVERGENCES.md:
float association and reduction identities, and NaN/tie behavior in MAX.
Dune compares the exact upstream-to-Tolk diff, including hashes of both
complete outputs. Reference generation never rewrites approved diffs; no source
normalization is used. See ../README.md for the review workflow.
